window.SPL_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"Build AI workflows in minutes, not hours","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html","text":"SPL runs Markdown documents. spl code fences hold exact, Python-like code; blockquotes hold natural-language agent commands. Both share one scope, the same tools and the same guards, so each step can be written in whichever form reads best. The loop is ordinary code; each ask must return JSON matching Card, with one repair attempt. The blockquote agent saves its answer through a shared-variable tool, and the assert after it checks the result. Writing is limited to ./out.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Why SPL?","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#why-spl","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"How a run works","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#how-a-run-works","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Non-goals","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#non-goals","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Where next","page":"Introduction","href":"index.html#where-next","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html","text":"Install the CLI, sign in to GitHub Copilot, then write and refine a workflow in a short edit-run loop.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Install","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#install","text":"SPL needs Node.js 20+. From a checkout, run npm install in lang/ and use node dist/cli.js in place of spl. spl login reuses existing credentials (Copilot CLI, gh, or COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN, GH_TOKEN, GITHUB_TOKEN). Otherwise it runs GitHub's device flow and saves a token in ~/.spl (override with SPL_HOME). spl logout deletes it. Workflows that never call a model, --check and replay need no login.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Write a workflow","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#write-a-workflow","text":"","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Document structure","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#document-structure","text":"Blocks run in document order in one shared scope. success, fail and a top-level return end the whole document.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"The iteration loop","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#the-iteration-loop","text":"Replay needs the same model requests in the same order. After changing a prompt, record a new trace. Explicit tool calls still run during replay; see traces and replay.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"VS Code extension","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#vs-code-extension","text":"The extension in vscode/ adds parser-backed diagnostics, completions, hover, go to definition, document symbols, highlighting for spl fences and @name markers, and Markdown preview highlighting. Type splworkflow for a new document.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Run the tests","page":"Getting started","href":"getting-started.html#run-the-tests","text":"Tests are offline. To include authenticated live tests in PowerShell, run $env:SPL_LIVE_TESTS='1'; npm test, then remove the variable.","group":"Start here"},{"title":"Language","page":"Language","href":"language.html","text":"SPL is a small Python-like language with brace-delimited blocks. This page covers the code inside spl fences: values, bindings, control flow, functions, built-ins and exact tools.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Values and strings","page":"Language","href":"language.html#values-and-strings","text":"Values are None, True/False, numbers, strings, lists, dicts with string keys, and functions. Statements end at a newline or ;. # starts a comment, and a trailing \\ continues a line. Escapes: \\n \\t \\r \\0 \\\\ \\' \\\" \\{ \\}. Python-style operators work as expected: + - * / // % **, comparisons, in/not in, and/or/not, a if cond else b, indexing, slicing with negative indexes, and d.key as a shorthand for d['key'].","group":"The language"},{"title":"Bindings and scopes","page":"Language","href":"language.html#bindings-and-scopes","text":"let declares a name in the current block; plain assignment updates the nearest existing declaration. Every block is a child scope, so inner let shadows outer names and does not leak out. Each loop iteration gets fresh variables. Declare a result before a block when the block should set it: let answer = None and then answer = ... inside.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Control flow and expressions","page":"Language","href":"language.html#control-flow-and-expressions","text":"if/elif/else, for, while, break, continue and pass behave as in Python, with braces. for k, v in items(d) unpacks pairs. Blocks and if are expressions that yield their last expression, or None. {} is an empty dict; { pass } is an empty block.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Functions","page":"Language","href":"language.html#functions","text":"def defines a function with lexical closures and recursion. Functions return None unless they use an explicit return.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Runtime inputs: $env and $args","page":"Language","href":"language.html#runtime-inputs-env-and-args","text":"$env.NAME or $env['NAME'] reads a process environment variable as a string. $args[0], $args[1], … read the CLI's repeated --arg values. Missing variables and out-of-range indexes return None. Both are read-only snapshots and are never persisted by stores.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Logging","page":"Language","href":"language.html#logging","text":"log(value, ...) writes to stderr at info level. log(level=debug)(value) selects error, info or debug. Debug output appears only with --log-level debug; --silent hides all logs. Objects and lists are logged as indented JSON, including when mixed with text arguments. Every continuation line carries the same log tag. Terminal output colors JSON tokens; redirected logs are plain text by default. See console formatting.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Built-in functions","page":"Language","href":"language.html#built-in-functions","text":"Function Description --- --- len(x) type(x) Length; type name str(x) int(x) float(x) bool(x) list(x) Conversions range(stop) range(start, stop[, step]) List of integers abs(x) round(x[, digits]) min(...) max(...) sum(list) Numbers sorted(list[, reverse]) reversed(list) enumerate(list) zip(a, b, ...) Sequences any(list) all(list) Truthiness checks keys(d) values(d) items(d) Dict views as lists json_parse(text) json_stringify(value[, indent]) Strict JSON log(...) Logging, see above Methods: Type Methods --- --- String upper lower strip lstrip rstrip title capitalize isdigit split splitlines join replace startswith endswith find count List append extend insert pop remove index count reverse sort copy clear Dict keys values items get pop update copy clear","group":"The language","signatures":["len(x)","type(x)","str(x)","int(x)","float(x)","bool(x)","list(x)","range(stop)","range(start, stop[, step])","abs(x)","round(x[, digits])","min(...)","max(...)","sum(list)","sorted(list[, reverse])","reversed(list)","enumerate(list)","zip(a, b, ...)","any(list)","all(list)","keys(d)","values(d)","items(d)","json_parse(text)","json_stringify(value[, indent])","log(...)"]},{"title":"Exact tools","page":"Language","href":"language.html#exact-tools","text":"Tools are ordinary functions that never call a model. Paths are relative to --cwd, and every call is checked against active restrictions. Function Result --- --- read_file(path) File contents as UTF-8 text write_file(path, content) Writes the file and returns a confirmation web_fetch(url) Response body as text (30 s timeout, up to 5 redirects) web_search(query) Search results as text shell(command) Combined stdout/stderr; a nonzero exit raises tool_error (120 s timeout) shell runs through the platform shell (cmd.exe on Windows, /bin/sh elsewhere). External state can still change between runs; \"exact\" means no model is involved.","group":"The language","signatures":["read_file(path)","write_file(path, content)","web_fetch(url)","web_search(query)","shell(command)"]},{"title":"Natural language & models","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html","text":"Blockquotes let you write a step in plain language; ask, think and agent call the model from code. This page covers both, and how to check what comes back.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Blockquotes","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#blockquotes","text":"A blockquote is an agent command, not code to compile into SPL. The model sees the instruction, visible bindings and function signatures, active guidance and restrictions, and the available tools. It acts directly on the shared document scope through tool calls. For a simple generation task, the comparable code is: Blockquotes have these tools in addition to configured MCP tools: Tool Purpose --- --- load_variable(name) Read a shared variable or read-only $args save_variable(name, value, schema?) Create or update a shared JSON value; optionally validate a JSON Schema before saving call_function(name, args) Invoke an existing SPL function or builtin with positional JSON arguments get_env(name) Read one read-only environment value; missing values return null ask_human(question) Pause through checkpoint/resume and return the human's answer read_file , write_file , web_fetch , web_search , shell Use the existing exact runtime tools restrict(kind, value) Narrow restrictions for the rest of the command, including function and MCP calls set_guidance(kind, text) Add enforce / forbid guidance for this command and its nested model calls success(value) , fail(reason) End the whole document These shared-scope tools are specific to blockquotes. Explicit agent expressions retain their existing runtime/MCP tool set; ask and think still have no tools. @name marks a binding or operation (@notes, @success, @read_file), not text substitution. Plain language works too. Common conventions guide the agent's tool choices: Marker Agent behavior --- --- @ENFORCE text / @FORBID text Follow prompt guidance; use set_guidance for nested model calls @RESTRICT fs['./src'] exec['git'] Narrow the command's restrictions with restrict @validate[Schema] @retries[2] Load Schema , save with schema validation, and correct rejected values without repeating actions @HUMAN question Call ask_human (needs --checkpoint ) @SUCCESS , @fail End the document Each runtime tool call yields control to the interpreter. It executes the action, supplies the real result or error, and then lets the agent continue. Saving a variable takes effect immediately; prose replies do not create bindings. Functions retain their normal lexical scope and runtime guards. Restriction violations and explicit failures stop the command. Other tool failures are logged and returned to the model for handling; completed external actions are not automatically retried. Follow important steps with an spl check: --check validates syntax only; it cannot validate a blockquote's intent. --trace records the agent's decisions and runtime tool results. Replay restores shared-variable changes and function execution without repeating agent-owned external effects or contacting MCP. Generation-only --record cannot record blockquotes. Run only trusted documents. Old traces containing submitted SPL are incompatible with agent commands; record a new trace. Human input uses the existing checkpoint workflow, never a terminal prompt. Completed decisions and effects are replayed from the journal after --resume; the agent then receives the human's answer and continues. It may make new model calls after the answer.","group":"The language","signatures":["load_variable(name)","save_variable(name, value, schema?)","call_function(name, args)","get_env(name)","ask_human(question)","read_file","write_file","web_fetch","web_search","shell","restrict(kind, value)","set_guidance(kind, text)","success(value)","fail(reason)","@ENFORCE text","@FORBID text","@RESTRICT fs['./src'] exec['git']","@validate[Schema] @retries[2]","@HUMAN question","@SUCCESS","@fail"]},{"title":"Generation: ask and think","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#generation-ask-and-think","text":"ask prompt returns the model's answer as a value. think is the same with medium reasoning by default. Neither has tools: they cannot read files, browse or run commands. Each call starts a fresh session. Pass context explicitly, usually through interpolation.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Agents","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#agents","text":"agent prompt lets the model act with the runtime tools (read_file, write_file, web_fetch, web_search, shell) and any configured MCP tools. Actions are checked against active restrictions. Agents default to medium reasoning and never use repairs, so a failed check never repeats their actions.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Modifiers","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#modifiers","text":"All three commands take named modifiers in parentheses before the prompt. Values are expressions, evaluated once and validated before the call. Unknown or duplicate names are syntax errors. Modifier Values Effect --- --- --- len word sentence paragraph long Length hint (guidance only) fmt see [Output formats](#output-formats) Parse and check the answer schema JSON Schema draft-07 Validate the answer; implies JSON repairs 0 – 5 Extra attempts after a format or schema error reasoning low medium high Reasoning effort, if the model supports it show True False Stream reasoning to the info log model model id Per-call model Model precedence: CLI --model, then the per-call model, then frontmatter model, then the SDK default.","group":"The language","signatures":["len","fmt","schema","repairs","reasoning","show","model"]},{"title":"Output formats","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#output-formats","text":"fmt Accepts --- --- text (default) Trimmed text, no parsing integer JSON number without a fraction float Any finite JSON number boolean JSON true or false array JSON array (a list) object JSON object (a dict, not an array) json Any JSON value Parsing is strict: prose around JSON and Markdown code fences are rejected, and values are never coerced. A malformed or wrong-typed answer raises invalid_format.","group":"The language","signatures":["text","integer","float","boolean","array","object","json"]},{"title":"Schemas and repairs","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#schemas-and-repairs","text":"schema validates the parsed answer with Ajv in strict mode: no coercion, no defaults, no removal of extra fields. A mismatch raises validation_failed with field paths. repairs=n allows up to n more attempts, each with the previous answer and the validation errors. Only invalid_format and validation_failed are repaired; transport and tool errors are not. The prompt is not re-evaluated. Put business rules in assert, after the call.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Guidance: enforce and forbid","page":"Natural language & models","href":"llm-commands.html#guidance-enforce-and-forbid","text":"enforce text { ... } and forbid text { ... } add instructions to every model call inside the block, including calls in functions it invokes. Nested guidance combines. Guidance shapes prompts; it does not check the answer.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Guards & failures","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html","text":"Model output varies, so SPL makes checks explicit: restrict limits actions, assert and schemas check data, failures can be caught, and human hands decisions to a person.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Restrictions","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#restrictions","text":"restrict limits what tools and agents may touch inside its block. Outside any restriction, tools and agents are allowed by default. A blocked action raises restriction_violation. Clause Allows --- --- restrict './out' or restrict fs './out' Files inside the folder only restrict net 'api.example.com' Only these hosts ( *.example.com matches subdomains) restrict exec 'npm,git' Only these executables restrict mcp 'docs/search' Only these MCP servers ( docs , docs/* ) or tools ( docs/search ) Values are a comma-separated string or a list of strings. Combine clauses with commas; nested or repeated clauses of the same kind intersect, so a scope can only narrow. What is checked: Models are told the active restrictions, and the runtime enforces them on every call. ask and think have no tools at all.","group":"The language","signatures":["restrict './out'","restrict fs './out'","restrict net 'api.example.com'","restrict exec 'npm,git'","restrict mcp 'docs/search'"]},{"title":"Assertions, fail and success","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#assertions-fail-and-success","text":"","group":"The language"},{"title":"Failure payload","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#failure-payload","text":"Every failure has the same shape. Uncaught failures are printed to stdout and exit with code 1. status_code Cause --- --- invalid_format Answer is not valid JSON of the requested fmt validation_failed Answer does not match the schema assertion_failed An assert was false explicit_failure fail was executed restriction_violation An action was blocked by restrict tool_error Missing file, HTTP failure or nonzero shell exit llm_error Authentication, timeout, transport or replay mismatch runtime_error Undefined name, wrong type, invalid modifier and similar bugs","group":"The language","signatures":["invalid_format","validation_failed","assertion_failed","explicit_failure","restriction_violation","tool_error","llm_error","runtime_error"]},{"title":"try / except","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#try--except","text":"The handler gets the failure payload as a dict. Failures do not roll back earlier writes, and syntax errors are reported before anything runs, so they cannot be caught.","group":"The language"},{"title":"Human in the loop","page":"Guards & failures","href":"safety.html#human-in-the-loop","text":"human 'question' asks a person and returns their exact answer as a string. It never reads the terminal. Instead the run saves its progress to a checkpoint journal, prints the question as JSON, and exits with code 3. A later invocation supplies the answer and continues without repeating completed tool or model calls. Blockquote agents use the ask_human tool for the same workflow. The answer is returned to the agent on resume, which may then make new model calls and save it to a shared variable. Runtime actions completed before the question are not repeated, including actions inside invoked functions. The pending question looks like this: Resume needs the same workspace, script, runtime, --model/--mcp options and environment. The journal is not a snapshot: files changed in between stay changed. If a run was interrupted mid-action, SPL refuses to guess and asks for manual recovery. Checkpoints cannot be combined with stores or trace/replay. Journals contain prompts and answers, so keep them private. In CI, treat exit code 3 as \"waiting\", publish the question to an authorized channel, and resume in the same persistent workspace once an answer arrives.","group":"The language"},{"title":"CLI & runtime","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html","text":"spl runs one Markdown workflow, prints its result on stdout and logs on stderr, and reports the outcome through its exit code.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Usage","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#usage","text":"Option Description --- --- --arg \u003cvalue> Script argument exposed as $args[i] ; repeatable, also --arg=value --model \u003cid> Model for every call, overriding per-call and frontmatter models --cwd \u003cdir> Base folder for paths, restrictions, tools and agents (default: current) --check Validate frontmatter, syntax and --mcp config without running --log-level \u003clevel> error , info (default) or debug --silent No logs; only the result --timeout \u003cs> Per model call (default 300) --mcp \u003cfile.json> MCP servers, see [MCP servers](#mcp-servers) --trace / --replay \u003cfile.jsonl> Record or replay model calls, see [Traces and replay](#traces-and-replay) --store \u003cfile.json> / --store-module \u003cpath> Persist top-level variables, see [Variable stores](#variable-stores) --checkpoint , --resume , --answer-for , --answer , --answer-file [Human input](Safety.md#human-in-the-loop) -v , --version / -h , --help Version / usage Each --arg is one string; use --arg=--value for values starting with a dash.","group":"Reference","signatures":["--arg \u003cvalue>","--model \u003cid>","--cwd \u003cdir>","--check","--log-level \u003clevel>","--silent","--timeout \u003cs>","--mcp \u003cfile.json>","--trace","--replay \u003cfile.jsonl>","--store \u003cfile.json>","--store-module \u003cpath>","--checkpoint","--resume","--answer-for","--answer","--answer-file","-v","--version","-h","--help"]},{"title":"Output and exit codes","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#output-and-exit-codes","text":"stdout gets the success value: strings as-is, everything else as JSON. An uncaught failure prints its payload instead. stderr gets logs, including every model prompt and response and a trace of executed statements. Use --silent when logs could expose sensitive data. Every log line uses [tag] message, including continuation lines and CLI diagnostics. Multiline payloads are indented under their header. JSON objects, arrays and JSON model responses use two-space indentation; JSON keys, strings, numbers, booleans and null have distinct colors. Script source traces are displayed as written rather than reformatted as JSON. Colors are enabled automatically when stderr is a terminal, and disabled for redirected output or TERM=dumb. Set NO_COLOR=1 to disable color, or FORCE_COLOR=1 to enable it when redirecting; FORCE_COLOR=0 disables color, and NO_COLOR takes precedence. stdout results, checkpoint envelopes, trace journals and variable stores remain plain, machine-readable data. --silent suppresses execution logs; usage, syntax and checkpoint-usage diagnostics still appear on stderr so command-line errors are not hidden. For example, log('review:', review) produces: Embedded callers can pass structured data directly with logger.info('review:', review). Custom logger sinks default to plain text; new Logger('info', sink, { color: true }) explicitly enables styling. Non-JSON values such as errors, bigints and circular objects use Node's inspect format instead of losing error details or failing JSON serialization. Code Meaning --- --- 0 Success 1 Script failure 2 Syntax or usage error 3 Waiting for human input 70 Internal or infrastructure error","group":"Reference","signatures":["0","1","2","3","70"]},{"title":"Traces and replay","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#traces-and-replay","text":"--trace writes every ask, think, agent and blockquote request with its raw response or failure to a new JSONL file. --replay feeds those results back without contacting Copilot or MCP servers, so you can iterate on code and guards for free. Requests must match exactly and in order (prompt, kind, model, reasoning); a mismatch, missing or unused entry fails. Replay is not a dry run: explicit read_file, write_file, shell and web calls, and stores, still run. Agent actions are not repeated, and their side effects are not recreated. Blockquote decisions rebuild shared bindings and run existing functions against recorded external-tool results. The older --record mode writes generation-only recordings; replaying them blocks writes, shell, network, agents (including blockquotes) and stores. Traces and recordings contain full prompts, answers and requested environment values, so keep them private.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Variable stores","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#variable-stores","text":"--store state.json loads top-level variables at start and saves them at exit, even after a failure. Functions and $env/$args are not saved. See journal.md. --store-module ./store.mjs uses a module exporting createStore(), which returns synchronous load() and save(vars) methods:","group":"Reference"},{"title":"MCP servers","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#mcp-servers","text":"--mcp takes a file in VS Code's mcp.json format. MCP tools are available only to agents. Use the server names in restrict mcp, and --check to validate the file without starting servers. Only load configurations you trust.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Models and authentication","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#models-and-authentication","text":"Every model call goes to GitHub Copilot through the Copilot SDK in a fresh session. See Getting started for spl login. A call without credentials fails with llm_error. The SDK has no temperature or seed, so live output is not reproducible. Contain variance with schemas, assertions and traces.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Embedding","page":"CLI & runtime","href":"cli.html#embedding","text":"run returns success (with value), failure (with failure), or waiting_for_input when given a Checkpoint. Options include backend, logger, cwd, args (default [], never process.argv), store, model and checkpoint. Wrap a backend in TraceBackend to record, or use ReplayBackend to replay; close it to check for unused entries. A custom backend implements call(request) and close(); only requests of kind agent may run tools. Raw parse and run still accept plain SPL source.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Grammar","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html","text":"The complete syntax of SPL documents and the code inside spl fences, in EBNF. Modifier names are checked while parsing; modifier values and schemas are checked at runtime.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Notation","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#notation","text":"{ x } repeats, [ x ] is optional, | separates alternatives. Statements end at a newline, ;, a closing } or the end of input. Newlines inside () and [] are ignored, and a trailing \\ continues a line. Opening braces must be on the same line as their keyword. # starts a comment.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Complete grammar","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#complete-grammar","text":"","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Maintaining the grammar","page":"Grammar","href":"grammar.html#maintaining-the-grammar","text":"This page is the only copy of the grammar. When the syntax changes, update it together with the VS Code TextMate grammar, then run npm run docs:build from the repository root. The documentation tests check that the rendered grammar and the example listings stay in sync.","group":"Reference"},{"title":"Examples","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html","text":"Every example is a runnable document in lang/examples. Run them from the lang folder, for example spl examples/hi.md. Each example's header shows its arguments.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Getting started","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#getting-started","text":"Example Shows --- --- [hi.md](../lang/examples/hi.md) Code and natural language sharing variables, a schema, an assertion and a restricted write [basics.md](../lang/examples/basics.md) Checking a model's answer against exact code; guidance with @ENFORCE and @FORBID [is-friday.md](../lang/examples/is-friday.md) Branching on a JSON answer [env.md](../lang/examples/env.md) Reading $env and --arg values [color-poem.md](../lang/examples/color-poem.md) Asking a person with @HUMAN (needs --checkpoint )","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Developer workflows","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#developer-workflows","text":"Example Shows --- --- [code-review.md](../lang/examples/code-review.md) A review that fails CI on high-severity findings [commit-message.md](../lang/examples/commit-message.md) Conventional commit from staged changes, format checks and human approval [release-notes.md](../lang/examples/release-notes.md) User-facing notes from git log prepended to CHANGELOG.md [fix-failing-tests.md](../lang/examples/fix-failing-tests.md) A bounded agent repair loop restricted to the project [triage-issues.md](../lang/examples/triage-issues.md) Labeling GitHub issues with gh after human approval","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Data and documents","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#data-and-documents","text":"Example Shows --- --- [extract-invoice.md](../lang/examples/extract-invoice.md) Structured extraction checked by arithmetic [translate-docs.md](../lang/examples/translate-docs.md) Translations verified to keep links, code and headings [incident-report.md](../lang/examples/incident-report.md) Exact log statistics, model-written narrative [web-digest.md](../lang/examples/web-digest.md) Web research with restricted fetches and checked citations","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Agents, state and people","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#agents-state-and-people","text":"Example Shows --- --- [mcp.md](../lang/examples/mcp.md) An agent limited to one MCP tool [journal.md](../lang/examples/journal.md) State across runs with --store [human-review.md](../lang/examples/human-review.md) Pausing for review with --checkpoint and resuming with the answer","group":"Guides","signatures":[]},{"title":"Extract an invoice","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#extract-an-invoice","text":"The schema checks the shape of the answer; plain arithmetic checks that the numbers add up.","group":"Guides"},{"title":"Triage issues","page":"Examples","href":"examples.html#triage-issues","text":"Exact gh calls fetch and label issues; the model only classifies, and nothing changes until a person approves.","group":"Guides"}];
